## Context

Status is a pure builder: it receives a project's files, every kept session's events, each session's liveness and the board, and turns them into what the page shows. It has nothing of its own to fall back on, so every choice about which input wins is made here.

The inputs disagree in predictable ways. Files are always current but say nothing about who or when. Reports say who and when but can be missing, late or from an earlier session. Liveness has already been judged by the recording side, and a second judgement here would let the page contradict the halt the agent just received. A pipeline's stages are spread across sessions, some ended or archived, and a stage the agent skipped looks exactly like one it forgot to report.

## Strategy

**Inputs as given; nothing judged twice.** Liveness, events and the board arrive judged and read; status never reads presence, logs or the board itself and never re-judges a session. One judgement means the page shows the same open session the start tool just halted on.

**Files outrank events for current state.** A phase or a waiting gate is confirmed against the file it concerns: a plan still proposed cannot be in Build, and a waiting file is surfaced only while it still awaits approval. A file that no longer exists surfaces nothing, so a missed report costs a history row, never a wrong screen.

**Stages fill from reports in a fixed order, and skipped needs a skip report.** A skip report makes a stage skipped; a finish in this or an earlier kept session of the same pipeline, a later phase starting, or its gate resolving makes it done; the current phase runs; the rest are pending. A gap is never read as a skip, because the agent's silence and its decision look the same.

**A running phase ends at the next boundary.** A phase stops running at the next phase of its pipeline, a commit or its session's end, so a missed finish report cannot leave Build spinning for days. Only live sessions get a rail; an ended session's rail is hidden rather than drawn with a guessed close.

**Which rail, and what hand work looks like.** The rail shows the session's current pipeline, else its last finished one until the next starts, and never mixes in another pipeline or a deleted session. With no phase running and only file writes or commits latest, it says the session is working outside the pipeline instead of showing a stale phase.

**Counted the cleanup way; failures stay local.** Checkbox progress uses the same count cleanup uses to refuse, so the page and the refusal agree. A malformed file, absent board or missing event empties only its own node or part.

## Good examples

**A resumed pipeline.** Yesterday's ended session finished Spec through Plan for a task; today's session starts that pipeline at Build. The rail shows Spec through Plan done from the kept log, Build running, QA and Fix pending.

**A missed finish.** Build was never reported finished, but the agent committed and started QA. Build reads done and QA running; no row claims Build finished at a guessed time.

**Hand work.** Between pipelines the agent edits a doc and commits. The rail reads working outside the pipeline, not the last pipeline's QA still running.

## Bad examples

**A skip from a gap.** Research was never reported in a pipeline that started at Plan with no skip reports, and the rail paints Research skipped. The user reads a decision nobody made.

**Liveness judged again.** Status applies its own quiet rule, shows the other session ended, and the user wonders why the agent halted on it.

**Build forever.** No phase-finished report arrived and nothing ends a running phase at a commit, so the rail shows Build running through QA, the fix round and the next day.

## References

- User decisions on session-scoped rails, resuming from kept logs with deleted sessions forgotten, skip reports, boundaries that end a running phase, and working outside the pipeline, relayed by the orchestrator (2026-11-25) -- stage, boundary and rail-choice decisions
- User answer that a just-ended session's rail is hidden, relayed by the orchestrator (2026-11-25) -- only live sessions get a rail
- User decisions on files deciding state, the surfaced waiting file and checkbox counts matching cleanup, relayed by the orchestrator (2026-11-22) -- files-outrank-events and counting decisions
